modern_restatement_en:
The map $T:M\mapsto(P/R,\,Q/R)$ is a projective collineation of the plane, since $P,Q,R$ are affine-linear in $(x,y)$. Let $C$ be a curve of degree $n$ not passing through the three fixed points of $T$. Prove:
(1) $T(C)$ has degree $n$;
(2) the lines $M\,T(M)$, $M\in C$, envelope a curve of class $2n$. Equivalently, its tangential equation has degree $2n$: through a general point $O$ pass $2n$ such lines, because $\det(O,M,TM)=0$ is a conic meeting $C$ in $2n$ points.
